  <!-- SECTION 6: RECOMMENDATIONS -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-number">6</div>
      <h2>Key Findings &amp; Strategic Recommendations</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="section-body">
      <p class="section-intro">Synthesizing findings across model, colorway, condition, channel, and temporal dimensions, the following recommendations reflect the highest-confidence patterns in the dataset for operators optimizing resale profitability and capital efficiency.</p>

      <ul class="rec-list">
        <li>
          <div class="rec-num">1</div>
          <div class="rec-content">
            <strong>Prioritize Deadstock condition exclusively for profit-positive outcomes</strong>
            <p>Every single Deadstock transaction in the dataset cleared above retail (100% rate, avg +$264.01). Every Used transaction closed below retail (0% rate, avg −$57.66). Condition is a binary profitability switch. Unless acquiring Used inventory at a steep discount below $132 resale average, avoid Used sourcing entirely. VNDS (83.3% premium rate, +$37.62 avg) is a viable secondary tier but requires higher selectivity.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div class="rec-num">2</div>
          <div class="rec-content">
            <strong>Focus sourcing on Air Jordan 11 Retro and Air Jordan 4 Retro silhouettes</strong>
            <p>These two models lead the dataset in avg profit margin ($99.84 and $95.76, respectively) and both maintain above-average premium rates (63.4% and 62.4%). The AJ11 trades at the highest price point ($327 avg resale) with the fastest inventory turn (21.4 days), making it the highest-efficiency model. The AJ1 Low should be deprioritized — its $50.52 avg margin is the lowest of all five models.</p>
